|~ The verbs of numbers [D62]. Each resides on `::qlang/number`, the
   kind whose module declares it, so its subject is a number, and its
   head is its declaration, which the runtime executes before the
   primitive runs [D72]. The refusals of a verb sit under it: the head
   raises the one its site declares at the place it checks, and the
   primitive the rest. ~|

:add
  |~~ Adds two numbers: `a | add b` computes a + b, and the full form
      `add a b` reads both modifiers against the value in the pipe.

      ~(10 | add 3 | eq 13)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | add /x /y | eq 13)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:addend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/add})

::AddLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ The subject of `add` must be a number.

      ~(
        "hello" | add 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::AddRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The addend of `add` must be a number.

      ~(
        1 | add "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::AddRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::AddResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `add` answered outside the finite-double domain a qlang
      Number lives in. Both operands are of the right kind
      and ride the descriptor as `:leftValue` / `:rightValue`; the
      magnitude is what the site refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | add 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::AddResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:sub
  |~~ Subtracts the subtrahend from the subject, the minuend.

      ~(10 | sub 3 | eq 7)
      ~({:x 10 :y 3} | sub /x /y | eq 7)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:subtrahend ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/sub})

::SubLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ The subject of `sub` must be a number.

      ~(
        "hello" | sub 1
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SubLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::SubRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The subtrahend of `sub` must be a number.

      ~(
        1 | sub "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::SubRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::SubResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `sub` answered outside the finite-double domain a qlang
      Number lives in. Both operands are of the right kind
      and ride the descriptor as `:leftValue` / `:rightValue`; the
      magnitude is what the site refuses.

      ~(
        -1e308 | sub 1e308
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::SubResultNotFiniteError -1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:mul
  |~~ Multiplies two numbers.

      ~(10 | mul 3 | eq 30)
      ~({:x 5 :y 4} | mul /x /y | eq 20)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:multiplier ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/mul})

::MulLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ The subject of `mul` must be a number.

      ~(
        "hello" | mul 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::MulLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::MulRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The multiplier of `mul` must be a number.

      ~(
        1 | mul "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::MulRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::MulResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `mul` answered outside the finite-double domain a qlang
      Number lives in. Both operands are of the right kind
      and ride the descriptor as `:leftValue` / `:rightValue`; the
      magnitude is what the site refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | mul 10
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::MulResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

:div
  |~~ Divides the subject, the dividend, by the divisor, and refuses a
      divisor of zero with `::DivisionByZeroError`.

      ~(10 | div 2 | eq 5)
      ~({:x 20 :y 4} | div /x /y | eq 5)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:divisor ::number | :returns ::number | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/div})

::DivLeftNotNumberError
  |~~ The subject of `div` must be a number.

      ~(
        "hello" | div 2
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DivLeftNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::DivRightNotNumberError
  |~~ The divisor of `div` must be a number.

      ~(
        1 | div "x"
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::DivRightNotNumberError ::string]
       )
  ~~|

::DivResultNotFiniteError
  |~~ `div` answered outside the finite-double domain a qlang
      Number lives in. Both operands are of the right kind
      and ride the descriptor as `:leftValue` / `:rightValue`; the
      magnitude is what the site refuses.

      ~(
        1e308 | div 0.0001
        !| [type /leftValue]
        |  eq [::DivResultNotFiniteError 1e308]
       )
  ~~|

::DivisionByZeroError
  |~~ Division by zero — `div _ 0` fires this error.

      ~(
        10 | div 0
        !| [type]
        |  eq [::DivisionByZeroError]
       )
  ~~|

:gt
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than the number the call gives.

      ~(10 | gt 5 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gt})

:gte
  |~~ Whether the subject is greater than or equal to the number the
      call gives.

      ~(10 | gte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/gte})

:lt
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than the number the call gives.

      ~(5 | lt 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lt})

:lte
  |~~ Whether the subject is less than or equal to the number the call
      gives.

      ~(10 | lte 10 | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::verb~(:than ::number | :returns ::boolean | ::builtin{:impl :qlang/prim/lte})
